Since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that

$$
\begin{aligned}T(kv+w)=T(kv)+T(w)=kT(v)+T(w)\end{aligned}
$$

for any vectors $v$ and $w.$ Therefore,

$$
\begin{aligned}T(2{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}) & =2T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2}) \\ \begin{bmatrix}11 \\ 3\end{bmatrix} & =2T({v}_{1})+\begin{bmatrix}5 \\ -19\end{bmatrix} \\ 2T({v}_{1}) & =\begin{bmatrix}11 \\ 3\end{bmatrix}-\begin{bmatrix}5 \\ -19\end{bmatrix} \\ 2T({v}_{1}) & =\begin{bmatrix}6 \\ 22\end{bmatrix} \\ T({v}_{1}) & =\frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix}6 \\ 22\end{bmatrix} \\ T({v}_{1}) & =\begin{bmatrix}3 \\ 11\end{bmatrix}.\end{aligned}
$$

Since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that

$$
\begin{aligned}T(v+kw)=T(v)+T(kw)=T(v)+kT(w)\end{aligned}
$$

for any vectors $v$ and $w.$ Therefore,

$$
\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}+3{v}_{2}) & =T({v}_{1})+3T({v}_{2}) \\ \begin{bmatrix}15 \\ 6\end{bmatrix} & =\begin{bmatrix}6 \\ -12\end{bmatrix}+3T({v}_{2}) \\ 3T({v}_{2}) & =\begin{bmatrix}15 \\ 6\end{bmatrix}-\begin{bmatrix}6 \\ -12\end{bmatrix} \\ 3T({v}_{2}) & =\begin{bmatrix}9 \\ 18\end{bmatrix} \\ T({v}_{2}) & =\frac{1}{3}\begin{bmatrix}9 \\ 18\end{bmatrix} \\ T({v}_{2}) & =\begin{bmatrix}3 \\ 6\end{bmatrix}.\end{aligned}
$$

