:question/worked-solution:
The Hadamard product of two matrices (or vectors) $A$ and $B$, denoted $A \odot B$, is computed by multiplying corresponding entries together elementwise:

$$
(A \odot B)_{ij} = a_{ij}b_{ij}
$$

Therefore, in this case, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\ 6 & 5 \\ 2 & 1\end{bmatrix}⊙\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\ -1 & 2 \\ 5 & -8\end{bmatrix} &= \begin{bmatrix}4 \cdot 2 & 3 \cdot 3 \\ 6 \cdot (-1) & 5 \cdot 2 \\ 2 \cdot 5 & 1 \cdot (-8)\end{bmatrix} \\
&= \begin{bmatrix}8 & 9 \\ -6 & 10 \\ 10 & -8\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}
$$

The Hadamard product of two compatible vectors $\mathbf{a}$ and $\mathbf{b}$ can be expressed as the matrix product

$$
\mathbf{a} \odot \mathbf{b} = \text{diag}(\mathbf{a})\mathbf{b}
$$

In our case, we have
$$
M\mathbf{u}^T
=
\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\ 3 & 4\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\ 5\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\ 14\end{bmatrix}
$$
and
$$
\mathbf{v} - \mathbf{u}^T
=
\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 3\end{bmatrix}
-
\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\ 5\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}3 \\ -2\end{bmatrix}.
$$

Notice that they are of the same size; hence, their Hadamard product is well-defined. We conclude that

$$
\begin{aligned}
Mu^{T}⊙(v - u^{T}) &= diag(Mu^{T})(v - u^{T}) \\
&= \begin{bmatrix}-9 & 0 \\ 0 & 14\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}3 \\ -2\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}
$$
